## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
<h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
<p style="margin: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning from Mistakes</td>
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Next Time, I Will Study Harder to Avoid...</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize audio and video content for study notes. Communication: Clarify misunderstood social cues in peer interactions.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize the main points from an educational video on learning from mistakes by drawing symbols (e.g., drawing a red "X" for a mistake and a checkmark for learning). Can clarify misunderstood social cues during discussions on personal growth by using facial expressions (e.g., showing "happy" for success and "sad" for a mistake). Can summarize stories about overcoming challenges, using visuals and gestures (e.g., thumbs up for improvement and drawing a ladder for progress).</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 44%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 23%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying two large symbols on the board: a red 'X' and a green checkmark. Students are asked what these symbols represent in the context of learning. The teacher then prompts students to recall a recent small challenge or "mistake" they made and one "improvement" they experienced, asking them to silently think about how they might summarize it using only these two symbols and simple gestures (e.g., shrugging for confusion, thumbs up for success). This connects to the theme "Next Time, I Will Study Harder to Avoid..." and activates prior knowledge about learning from experiences.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a personal experience using the symbols and gestures. For example, "Last week, I <em>forgot</em> my book for class (shows 'X' and a sad face). But then, I <em>learned</em> to pack my bag the night before (shows checkmark and a happy face). Next time, I <em>will</em> prepare more carefully." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of past tense verbs ending in /t/ (practiced), /d/ (improved), and /ɪd/ (wanted) as they describe potential experiences. The teacher also demonstrates using facial expressions (e.g., furrowed brow for confusion, bright eyes for understanding) to clarify meaning.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I show an 'X' symbol, does it mean a good thing or a challenge?" (Expected: "A challenge.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I show a checkmark and a happy face, what does it mean I did?" (Expected: "You learned something" or "You improved.")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short (2-3 minute) educational video titled "Common Study Mistakes and How to Fix Them" or "Learning from Academic Challenges." The video uses a clear narrative with simple animations or visual aids. The register is informative and encouraging.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student summarizes the main points from an educational video on learning from mistakes by drawing symbols.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pre-watching:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary from the video: <em>mistakes, grades, study habits, feedback, improvement, success</em>. Students predict one mistake they think the video will mention.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>While-watching (Gist):</b> Students watch the video once. Their task is to identify 2-3 main mistakes mentioned. They can jot down keywords or draw a simple 'X' next to their notes for each mistake.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Post-watching (Information matching):</b> Students watch the video a second time. This time, they note down one solution or "improvement" suggested for each mistake they identified. They can draw a checkmark next to each solution. The teacher pauses the video if needed for clarification. Students then briefly share with a partner, mediating the information they understood from the video by pointing to their symbols and saying, for example, "The video talked about (X) not planning homework. It suggested (✓) creating a study schedule."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Summarizing information using past simple for challenges, future tense for action plans, and "should" for advice. Clarifying meaning using gestures and facial expressions. Vocabulary: <em>mistakes, study, habits, homework, feedback, progress, improvement, success, practice, improve, try, learn, better, well</em>. Pronunciation of past tense verbs /d/, /ɪd/, /t/ is reinforced.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice – "Mistake & Solution Match":</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentence halves. One half describes a common academic mistake (e.g., "I <em>didn't study</em> for the English test."), and the other half describes an improvement plan or advice (e.g., "Next time, I <em>will practice</em> my vocabulary well." or "You <em>should</em> ask questions when you are unsure."). Students match the sentences. As they match, they orally read their completed sentences to a partner, making sure to use clear pronunciation for past tense verbs.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice – "Emoji Storytelling":</b> The teacher reads short scenarios about a student facing a challenge and then overcoming it. For each scenario, students work in pairs. First, they use facial expressions (e.g., sad/confused) and a simple gesture (e.g., shaking head for 'mistake') to represent the challenge. Then, they use a different facial expression (e.g., happy/determined) and gesture (e.g., thumbs up for 'success') to represent the learning/improvement. Finally, they quickly summarize the learning point using past and future tenses (e.g., "He <em>forgot</em> his homework. Next time, he <em>will remember</em> to pack it.") and provide advice ("He <em>should</em> make a checklist."). The teacher monitors for appropriate use of gestures and pronunciation, especially for minimal pairs if naturally occurring words like "they/day" or "think/sink" come up in student responses.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction – "Journal Summary Prep":</b> Students are introduced to the "Growth Mindset Journal" project. They look at a sample journal entry (not their own) that describes a challenge and a learning point. In pairs, they discuss how they would summarize this entry for someone else using 2-3 key symbols or simple drawings (e.g., a mountain for challenge, an arrow up for progress). They practice verbally explaining their chosen symbols, using phrases like "This symbol represents..." or "I drew this to show..." This helps them prepare to mediate their own journal content later.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Growth Mindset Journal Explanation Station"</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4). Each student will take a turn selecting one entry from their "Growth Mindset Journal" to share with their group. Their task is to mediate the key points of their chosen journal entry. They must summarize the challenge they <em>faced</em> (using past simple) and what they <em>learned</em> or what they <em>will do</em> next time (using future tense). Crucially, they must use at least two mediation techniques: a visual aid (e.g., drawing symbols like an 'X' for a mistake and a checkmark for learning, or a ladder for progress, directly from their journal or on a mini-whiteboard) and a non-verbal cue (facial expressions or gestures like thumbs up for improvement) to clarify their message for their peers. After each student presents, their group members will offer constructive feedback, using phrases like "You <em>should</em> include more details about..." or "I understood that you <em>tried</em> your best."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This task directly connects to the theme "Next Time, I Will Study Harder to Avoid..." and the scenario "Learning from Mistakes" by having students articulate and explain their personal growth journeys. The focus is on fluent communication, collaborative interaction, and effective mediation of personal experiences using multiple modalities.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment: Peer Observation & Teacher Checklist</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will present one summarized journal entry to a peer. The presenter will summarize the challenge and the learning, using simple symbols (e.g., drawing a red 'X' for a mistake, a checkmark for improvement) and gestures/facial expressions to clarify. The peer will use a simple checklist to assess the presenter's ability to mediate.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Presenter summarized the main points from their journal using past/future tenses (e.g., "I <em>forgot</em> my book," "Next time, I <em>will study</em> harder."). (Yes/No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Presenter effectively used symbols/gestures/facial expressions to clarify the message. (Yes/No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Presenter used target vocabulary (e.g., <em>mistakes, improvement, study, learn, feedback</em>) and "should" for advice. (Yes/No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Presenter's pronunciation of past tense verbs was generally intelligible. (Yes/No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Peer provided constructive feedback (e.g., "You <em>should</em> explain that part more clearly."). (Yes/No)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about summarizing your learning for others today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using symbols or gestures help you explain your ideas clearly?"</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new strategies did you learn today to explain your ideas or notes more clearly to others?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use symbols, drawings, or gestures in other school subjects or even outside of school to help you understand or explain complex information?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson regarding students' ability to mediate their learning experiences (summarizing, clarifying with visuals/gestures)? Did students effectively use the target grammar (past simple, future tense, 'should') and vocabulary?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, especially for students who struggled to integrate visuals or non-verbal cues? Were the minimal pair sounds clear enough?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This lesson concludes the "Learning from Mistakes" theme. How can these mediation skills, particularly in summarizing and giving feedback, be applied to upcoming themes or projects requiring collaboration and communication?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> (As this is Lesson 5, it marks the end of the current theme. The reflection focuses on applying these mediation skills more broadly.) The skills practiced in mediating personal growth stories and giving constructive feedback will be vital for future collaborative projects and presentations in upcoming themes, allowing students to explain complex ideas and support their peers more effectively.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>